@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:global.component.tabs a ds:Component;
    ds:name "Tabs";
    ds:summary "A tabs component organizes related content into separate sections, allowing users to switch between different content panels within the same context by clicking on labeled tab headers. It helps reduce visual clutter by displaying only one content panel at a time while keeping all options easily accessible.";
    ds:anatomyDsl "---\n# Authored 2026-09-13 from @canonical/react-ds-global src/lib/component/Tabs/styles.css on .ds.tabs\n# A horizontal strip of tabs with no gap between them over a full-width muted\n# bottom border; the strip fills its container and scrolls on overflow.\nnode:\n  uri: global.component.tabs\n  styles:\n    layout.type: block\n  edges:\n    - node:\n        role: tab list\n        styles:\n          layout.type: flex\n          layout.direction: row\n          # No gap between tabs (Figma).\n          spacing.gap: 0\n          spacing.external.block.start: 0\n          spacing.external.block.end: 0\n          spacing.external.inline.start: 0\n          spacing.external.inline.end: 0\n          spacing.internal.block.start: 0\n          spacing.internal.block.end: 0\n          spacing.internal.inline.start: 0\n          spacing.internal.inline.end: 0\n          layout.listStyle: none\n          # The baseline the active tab paints its highlighted segment over.\n          # Its width reads --dimension-strokeThickness-medium, a spelling no\n          # symbol declares (the declared one is --dimension-stroke-thickness-medium),\n          # and the roster has no key for a block-end border width anyway.\n          appearance.border.bottom.color: color.border.muted\n          layout.overflow.inline: auto\n        edges:\n          - node:\n              role: tab\n            relation:\n              cardinality: \"1..*\"\n              slotName: default\n      relation:\n        cardinality: \"1\"\n        slotName: default";
    ds:anatomyClassic "Styling\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=4388-106316&t=mrOkyDfhQY9JZwMz-1";
    ds:usage "Tabs are used to organize related content at the same level of hierarchy, allowing users to focus on one section at a time without leaving the current context. They work best when content sections are closely related but mutually exclusive (when users typically need to see only one section to complete their task).\n\nUnlike navigation, which moves users between different pages or sections of an application, tabs keep users in the same place while switching between different aspects of the same topic or dataset. This distinction is important: tabs are a content organization pattern, not a navigation pattern. The tab labels should be predictable and not overlap in content, giving users a clear mental model of what content lives under each tab. For example, tabs labeled \"Overview,\" \"Specifications,\" and \"Reviews\" for a product page set clear expectations about what each section contains.\n\nUnlike [Accordion](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-20dWwIHYhx/_rui-FfEcibePe1) , which stack vertically and can display multiple sections simultaneously, tabs enforce a single-focus model where only one content panel is ever visible. Choose [Accordion](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-20dWwIHYhx/_rui-FfEcibePe1) when users may need to compare information across sections or reference multiple sections at once. Choose tabs when the content is mutually exclusive and users benefit from focusing on one thing at a time.\n\n## \n\n### When to use\n\n- To organize related content that doesn't need to be viewed simultaneously\n- When you have 2–7 sections of content that are at the same level of hierarchy\n- To reduce cognitive load by breaking up content into digestible chunks\n- For switching between different aspects or properties of a single item (e.g., Overview, Specifications, Reviews for a product)\n- When space is limited and you need to display multiple categories of information in the same area\n- To group related settings or configuration options that are independent of each other\n\n### When not to use\n\n- As primary navigation. Tabs are for content organization, not for moving between pages. \n  - Incorrect: Using tabs in a main header to navigate the user to entirely different pages or unrelated views.\n  - Remediation: Use [Navigation](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-20dWwIHYhx/_rui-nkDiZdUMkV) to direct users to new pages.\n- Within forms where submission scope is unclear. Users won't know if all tabs or just the visible tab will be submitted. \n  - Incorrect: Placing a multi-part form across multiple tabs and having a single submit button at the bottom, confusing users about whether hidden fields are being saved. \n  - Remediation: Use a stepper or wizard pattern to guide the user through a clear, linear submission process.\n- For sequential or step-by-step processes. \n  - Incorrect: Guiding users through an onboarding or checkout process using tabs where the chronological order of operations is not well-defined. \n  - Remediation: Use a stepper or wizard pattern to represent chronological progression.\n- When users need to compare content across sections. \n  - Incorrect: Hiding comparative specifications across different tabs, forcing the user to click back and forth to evaluate differences. \n  - Remediation: Consider using a pattern that allows multiple sections to be viewed or expanded simultaneously, such as [Card](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-20dWwIHYhx/_rui-Gm_-gu8fGD)s.\n- For a single section of content. Tabs require at least two sections to be meaningful. \n  - Incorrect: Displaying only one tab containing all the page content, which serves no organizational purpose and adds unnecessary UI clutter. \n  - Remediation: Remove the tab structure and display the content directly on the page surface using standard headings and paragraphs.\n- When you have many sections (8+).  \n  - Incorrect: Cluttering the interface with too many tabs that wrap onto multiple lines and overwhelm the user. \n  - Remediation: Consider breaking the content into separate pages or using a dropdown/sidebar navigation instead.\n- To show different views of the same data/content. \n  - Incorrect: Using tabs to switch between a list view and a map view of the exact same location data. \n  - Remediation: Use [SegmentedControlField](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-20dWwIHYhx/_rui-q1-jZEKyxy) to indicate that the user is changing the view, not the content category.\n\n### Placement and context\n\nTabs should not appear at the very top of a page. Placing them there essentially creates another layer of navigation, which confuses users about where they are in the application hierarchy. If you find yourself wanting top-level tabs, consider whether those sections belong in the primary navigation instead.\n\nThere should always be content above the tabs that establishes context. This could be a page title, a description, or information about the item the tabs relate to. The tabs then organize the detailed content that follows. This placement reinforces that tabs are subdividing content within a page, not defining the page itself.\n\n### URL structure\n\nConsider whether users will want to reference a specific tab in the future, either for themselves or to share with others. If so, each tab should have its own URL. In marketing websites or informational pages, users rarely need to link directly to a specific tab. However, in applications where users collaborate or need to bookmark specific views, such as machine details, customer records, or project settings, deep linking to individual tabs becomes valuable. A technician sending a colleague to the \"Networking\" tab of a specific machine should be able to share a direct link.\n\n### Scope of effect\n\nTabs affect all content below them. This is the expected behavior and requires no additional visual treatment. However, there are cases where tabs appear in the middle of a page and only control a specific section rather than everything beneath them. In these situations, the affected area must be clearly distinguishable through visual boundaries, background colors, or contained card layouts, so users understand exactly what will change when they switch tabs.\n\nTabs cannot be nested. If you find yourself wanting tabs within tabs, reconsider your information architecture. This usually indicates that the content structure is too complex and would benefit from a different approach, such as separate pages or a combination of navigation and tabs.";
    ds:guidelines "";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:documentationStage ds:tag.needsdefinition;
    ds:hasTokenBinding [
        a ds:TokenBinding;
        ds:viaBlock ds:global.component.tabs;
        ds:node "$root/tab list";
        anatomy:styleKey "appearance.border.bottom.color";
        anatomy:styleState "default";
        ds:rank 1;
        ds:consumesSymbol dt:color.border.muted
    ].
